<root>
	<!--
	This file contains information about what models can be used as player
	models. Player models use the avatar model system and so all model files
	referenced must be included in the file avatar_model_data.xml

	The structure of the player model is such that a base model is chosen
	which will then have a list of available customisations.
	Customisations can be either model customisations or material
	customisations. One option is chosen from each customisation.

	A material customisation contains a list of tint options to be applied to a
	set of material groups(aka matters). Note that the material group must have
	at least one tint in Model Editor to be used in a material customisation.

	Example:
	<materialCustomisation>
		<name>	Pants Colour		</name>
		<materialGroups>
			<materialGroup>		Legs_skinned	</materialGroup>
		</materialGroups>
		<tints>
			<tint>
				<name>	Red Pants	</name>
				<tintName>	Default	</tintName>
			</tint>
			<tint>
				<name>	Merchant Pants	</name>
				<tintName>	Merchant	</tintName>
			</tint>
		</tints>
	</materialCustomisation>

	Model customisations contain a list of model options in the same format as
	the base model. They may include additional customisations possibilities 
	that only exist when that model is selected.

	Example:
	<modelCustomisation>
		<name>	Head	</name>
		<models>
			<model>
				<name>	Warrior Hair - Short	</name>
				<models>
					<resPath>	characters/avatars/warrior/hair.model </resPath>
				</models>
				<customisations>
				</customisations>
			</model>
			<model>
				<name>	Warrior Hair - Long	</name>
				<models>
					<resPath>	characters/avatars/warrior/hair00.model </resPath>
				</models>
				<customisations>
				</customisations>
			</model>
		</models>
	</modelCustomisation>
	-->

